          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 4: Catalyst deactivation and conversion",
          "content": "# Laboratory 4: Catalyst deactivation and conversion\n\n**Scientific focus:** Model intrinsic activity and outlet conversion for a first-order ideal reactor.\n\n## Governing model\n\n`a=exp(-k_d t); X=1-exp(-a k\u03c4)`\n\nThe implementation plots the stated analytical or numerical model while the learner changes input conditions. The controls are bounded to avoid obviously nonphysical operations such as negative concentrations, zero viscosity or a pressure-loss parameter outside the model's intended domain. The two series represent **Catalyst activity** and **Reactor conversion**. The horizontal variable is **Time on stream (h)**.\n\n## Investigation procedure\n\n1. Record the initial parameters and graph before changing an input. Identify one limiting prediction from the displayed equation.\n2. Vary one parameter at a time from its low to its high setting. Explain the direction of the first plotted response using the balance or transport resistance.\n3. Repeat the experiment with a second variable while restoring the first. Compare absolute changes and dimensionless changes rather than relying on visual slope alone.\n4. Use the **Export CSV** control and independently reproduce one point from the given equation to four significant digits.\n5. Write down a measurement you would need before applying this idealized relation to a real reactor.\n\n## Interpretation prompt\n\nShow why a large intrinsic activity decline may initially cause only a modest conversion decline in a high-conversion reactor.\n\n**Engineering limitations:** Educational curves are generated with the explicit assumptions printed above. They are not validated process simulator outputs, physical property correlations across arbitrary conditions, or substitutes for hazard analysis and code-compliant industrial design."
