          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Catalyst Deactivation and Kinetic Characterization",
          "content": "# Unit 2 Assessment \u2014 Catalyst Deactivation and Kinetic Characterization\n\n**16 topic-specific questions.** Provide equations, units, diagrams, uncertainty and interpretation rather than only one final number.\n\n**Question 1.** For poisoning and reversible inhibition, derive or interpret `a(t)=exp(-k_d t)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 2.** Design an experiment to distinguish poison adsorption; reversible coverage; permanent site loss from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 3.** For the scenario: During a 12-hour test, collect rate and temperature together; compare activity estimates based on normalized intrinsic rates instead of merely averaging conversion values. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 4.** Evaluate the restriction: Do not extrapolate a short-time exponential activity model through an unobserved regeneration cycle. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 5.** For sintering, coke growth and pore blockage, derive or interpret `1/a - 1=k_d t` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 6.** Design an experiment to distinguish structural changes, active area and diffusion deterioration from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 7.** For the scenario: Compare decay at two space velocities. If the apparent loss differs strongly, first test whether internal diffusion has changed before concluding the intrinsic deactivation rate changed. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 8.** Evaluate the restriction: Surface area, dispersion and mass-transfer properties should be characterized separately when possible. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 9.** For time-on-stream reactor performance, derive or interpret `X(t)=1-exp[-a(t) k tau]` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 10.** Design an experiment to distinguish conversion with evolving kinetic activity from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 11.** For the scenario: Model a fresh reactor with k tau=4 and activity falling from one to 0.5; quantify the loss in rate capacity and the much smaller apparent change in conversion. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 12.** Evaluate the restriction: Conversion near unity is a poor sole diagnostic of catalyst deterioration. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 13.** For regeneration and experimental design, derive or interpret `a_after=min(1,a_before+f_recovered(1-a_before))` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 14.** Design an experiment to distinguish regen cycle, uncertainty and kinetic identifiability from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 15.** For the scenario: Design an A/B sequence of fresh-run, poison-run, purge and regeneration; record repeat reference points and use a blank run to isolate measurement drift. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 16.** Evaluate the restriction: Regeneration can be hazardous and should be performed only under approved equipment-specific procedures. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n## Marking guide\n\nEach answer receives credit for (i) a stated physical model and boundary (25%), (ii) correct balance and dimensional consistency (30%), (iii) justified parameter handling and numerical or graphical evidence (25%), and (iv) limitations and safety reasoning (20%). Students should not earn full marks for a numerical answer with no assumptions or units."
