          "content": "# Unit 3 Assessment \u2014 Intraparticle Diffusion and Reaction\n\n**16 topic-specific questions.** Provide equations, units, diagrams, uncertainty and interpretation rather than only one final number.\n\n**Question 1.** For pore diffusivity and governing balance, derive or interpret `D_eff=(epsilon/tau_p) D_p` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 2.** Design an experiment to distinguish porosity, tortuosity and molecular versus Knudsen diffusion from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 3.** For the scenario: Measure effective diffusivity from an inert tracer experiment and compare with a reaction-rate-derived fit. Explain why two estimates may differ if the reacting species adsorbs strongly. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 4.** Evaluate the restriction: The simple porosity-over-tortuosity closure is a model and may fail in hierarchical or highly anisotropic pores. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 5.** For slab effectiveness and thiele modulus, derive or interpret `phi=L sqrt(k/D_eff); eta=tanh(phi)/phi` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 6.** Design an experiment to distinguish first-order plane-sheet diffusion and reaction from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 7.** For the scenario: Change the half-thickness from 1 mm to 4 mm while keeping intrinsic kinetics and diffusivity fixed; examine how the effectiveness factor and total catalyst utilization respond. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 8.** Evaluate the restriction: The classical formula assumes isothermal first-order kinetics and constant effective diffusivity. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 9.** For spherical pellet effectiveness, derive or interpret `eta=3(phi*coth(phi)-1)/phi^2` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 10.** Design an experiment to distinguish radial concentration profiles and utilization from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 11.** For the scenario: Compare equal-volume slabs and spheres at the same characteristic diffusional time, then discuss why geometry definitions must be consistent before comparing effectiveness. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 12.** Evaluate the restriction: Avoid interpreting the eta formula as a universal empirical catalyst correlation. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 13.** For combined internal and external resistances, derive or interpret `k_g(C_bulk-C_s)=eta k_v C_s V_p/A_p` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 14.** Design an experiment to distinguish film concentration and internal reaction rate from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 15.** For the scenario: Vary particle diameter and superficial velocity separately; plot bulk-to-surface and surface-to-center concentration differences and identify the dominant resistance. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 16.** Evaluate the restriction: Do not double count external mass transfer inside an experimentally fitted apparent-rate constant. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n## Marking guide\n\nEach answer receives credit for (i) a stated physical model and boundary (25%), (ii) correct balance and dimensional consistency (30%), (iii) justified parameter handling and numerical or graphical evidence (25%), and (iv) limitations and safety reasoning (20%). Students should not earn full marks for a numerical answer with no assumptions or units."
