          "id": "sec-4-9",
          "secNumber": "4.9",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Packed-Bed Catalytic Reactor Design (Ergun Pressure Drop)",
          "content": "# Unit 4 Assessment \u2014 Packed-Bed Reactor Design and Pressure Drop\n\n**16 topic-specific questions.** Provide equations, units, diagrams, uncertainty and interpretation rather than only one final number.\n\n**Question 1.** For catalyst-weight-based design, derive or interpret `dF_A/dW=r_A_prime` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 2.** Design an experiment to distinguish molar flow derivative and bed mass from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 3.** For the scenario: Calculate the catalyst weight for 70 percent conversion at constant volumetric flow and compare an analytic first-order expression against numerical integration. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 4.** Evaluate the restriction: Check whether kinetic parameters are defined per catalyst mass or per reactor volume. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 5.** For ergun pressure losses, derive or interpret `-dP/dz=150 mu(1-epsilon)^2 u/(epsilon^3 d_p^2)+1.75 rho(1-epsilon)u^2/(epsilon^3 d_p)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 6.** Design an experiment to distinguish viscous and inertial contributions to bed friction from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 7.** For the scenario: Hold bed depth and superficial velocity fixed, vary pellet size from 2 to 6 mm, and compare laminar and inertial pressure-loss terms. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 8.** Evaluate the restriction: Ergun coefficients describe packed-bed approximations, not arbitrary two-phase flow. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 9.** For variable density and gas-phase flow, derive or interpret `C_A=C_A0(1-X) (P/P0)(T0/T)/(1+epsilon X)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 10.** Design an experiment to distinguish gas expansion, pressure coupling and partial pressures from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 11.** For the scenario: Model an isothermal A\u21922B reaction while pressure declines and compare its outlet with constant-pressure assumptions. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 12.** Evaluate the restriction: Gas-phase mole expansion and temperature changes must not be omitted when materially important. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 13.** For design with uncertainty and constraints, derive or interpret `maximize productivity subject to DeltaP<=DeltaP_max` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 14.** Design an experiment to distinguish rate data, bed sizing and competing objectives from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 15.** For the scenario: Propagate upper and lower estimates of rate constant and inlet flow through the bed-size equation, then select a conservative design basis. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 16.** Evaluate the restriction: Model extrapolation beyond measured rate, pressure and temperature ranges warrants new experiments. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n## Marking guide\n\nEach answer receives credit for (i) a stated physical model and boundary (25%), (ii) correct balance and dimensional consistency (30%), (iii) justified parameter handling and numerical or graphical evidence (25%), and (iv) limitations and safety reasoning (20%). Students should not earn full marks for a numerical answer with no assumptions or units."
