          "id": "sec-5-9",
          "secNumber": "5.9",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Thermal Behavior of Catalytic Reactors",
          "content": "# Unit 5 Assessment \u2014 Thermal Behavior of Catalytic Reactors\n\n**16 topic-specific questions.** Provide equations, units, diagrams, uncertainty and interpretation rather than only one final number.\n\n**Question 1.** For adiabatic temperature rise, derive or interpret `dT/dX=(-DeltaH_r) F_A0/sum(F_i Cp_i)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 2.** Design an experiment to distinguish reaction heat, heat capacities and conversion from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 3.** For the scenario: Estimate the adiabatic temperature rise for an irreversible reaction with a known heat release, then repeat at half the inert dilution. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 4.** Evaluate the restriction: Cp may depend on temperature; use integrated enthalpy relations over broad ranges. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 5.** For heated catalyst beds and hot spots, derive or interpret `dT/dW=[(-DeltaH_r)(-r_A_prime)-UA_W(T-T_c)]/C_flow` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 6.** Design an experiment to distinguish competing Arrhenius acceleration and cooling from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 7.** For the scenario: Simulate a cooled packed bed over increasing coolant-side conductance and locate the highest axial temperature rather than only the outlet temperature. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 8.** Evaluate the restriction: Simplified one-dimensional models may miss radial thermal gradients and channeling. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 9.** For thermal multiplicity and ignition, derive or interpret `Q_gen(T)=Q_rem(T)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 10.** Design an experiment to distinguish nonlinear steady-state heat generation and cooling from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 11.** For the scenario: Plot a temperature-dependent reaction heat curve with a linear coolant removal curve; find intersections and classify them using the local net-heat slope. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 12.** Evaluate the restriction: Graphical roots depend on the fitted kinetics and heat-transfer model; no root search is a safety certification. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 13.** For interstage cooling and runaway prevention, derive or interpret `T_after_cool=T_before - Q_removed/(m_dot Cp)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 14.** Design an experiment to distinguish multiple beds, quenches and temperature limits from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 15.** For the scenario: Compare two adiabatic catalyst stages with an interstage temperature reduction and calculate the changed peak temperature and expected catalyst requirement. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 16.** Evaluate the restriction: Never use classroom simulations to set interlock or relief-system requirements. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n## Marking guide\n\nEach answer receives credit for (i) a stated physical model and boundary (25%), (ii) correct balance and dimensional consistency (30%), (iii) justified parameter handling and numerical or graphical evidence (25%), and (iv) limitations and safety reasoning (20%). Students should not earn full marks for a numerical answer with no assumptions or units."
