          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Fluidized-Bed and Multiphase Gas-Liquid Reactors",
          "content": "# Unit 6 Assessment \u2014 Fluidized-Bed and Multiphase Reactors\n\n**16 topic-specific questions.** Provide equations, units, diagrams, uncertainty and interpretation rather than only one final number.\n\n**Question 1.** For minimum fluidization velocity, derive or interpret `Ar=g d_p^3 rho_f(rho_p-rho_f)/mu^2` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 2.** Design an experiment to distinguish particle force balances and incipient suspension from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 3.** For the scenario: Estimate minimum fluidization for two particle sizes and compare their sensitivity to density and gas viscosity. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 4.** Evaluate the restriction: Empirical correlations require correct particle-shape and Reynolds-number applicability. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 5.** For bubbling and gas bypass, derive or interpret `X=1-[f_b exp(-k tau_b)+(1-f_b) exp(-k tau_e)]` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 6.** Design an experiment to distinguish two-phase fluidized-bed approximations from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 7.** For the scenario: Vary bubble bypass fraction at fixed intrinsic kinetics and show how residence-time distribution changes apparent reactor performance. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 8.** Evaluate the restriction: A two-path model is instructional rather than predictive for a particular commercial fluidized bed. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 9.** For trickle beds and gas\u2013liquid\u2013solid transport, derive or interpret `r_obs=eta_w k C_s/(1+k/k_La)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 10.** Design an experiment to distinguish wetting efficiency, transfer resistances and holdup from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 11.** For the scenario: Compare performance at low and high wetting fractions and identify whether a catalyst change or hydrodynamic change would improve conversion more. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 12.** Evaluate the restriction: Real wetting efficiency is spatially nonuniform and cannot be assigned confidently from liquid rate alone. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 13.** For stirred gas\u2013liquid reaction systems, derive or interpret `dC/dt=k_La(C_star-C)-k_rxn C` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 14.** Design an experiment to distinguish kLa measurement, dissolved reactant and consumption from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 15.** For the scenario: Compare dissolved concentration trajectories at three agitation rates for the same chemistry, then compute steady uptake and reaction rate. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 16.** Evaluate the restriction: Volumetric transfer coefficients depend on impeller, gas rate, geometry and fluid properties. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n## Marking guide\n\nEach answer receives credit for (i) a stated physical model and boundary (25%), (ii) correct balance and dimensional consistency (30%), (iii) justified parameter handling and numerical or graphical evidence (25%), and (iv) limitations and safety reasoning (20%). Students should not earn full marks for a numerical answer with no assumptions or units."
