          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Fluid-Solid Reactions and Shrinking Core Models",
          "content": "# Unit 7 Assessment \u2014 Fluid\u2013Solid and Specialized Reaction Systems\n\n**16 topic-specific questions.** Provide equations, units, diagrams, uncertainty and interpretation rather than only one final number.\n\n**Question 1.** For shrinking-core solid conversion, derive or interpret `t/tau_D=1-3(1-X)^(2/3)+2(1-X)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 2.** Design an experiment to distinguish film, ash-layer diffusion and chemical-reaction resistance from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 3.** For the scenario: For one pellet radius, compare a film-controlled linear relation with the diffusion-controlled shrinking-core relation and estimate the time to 80 percent conversion. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 4.** Evaluate the restriction: Particle cracking, swelling and changing porosity break the ideal shrinking-core assumption. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 5.** For solid particle populations, derive or interpret `X_total=sum_i(w_i X_i)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 6.** Design an experiment to distinguish particle size distributions and batch conversion from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 7.** For the scenario: Create a three-bin particle population with mass fractions and particle diameters; compare overall conversion with a monodisperse model at the mean diameter. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 8.** Evaluate the restriction: Mass-weighted and number-weighted particle-size distributions are different. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 9.** For enzyme catalytic reactors, derive or interpret `r=Vmax C/(Km+C)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 10.** Design an experiment to distinguish Michaelis\u2013Menten kinetics, inhibition and immobilization from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 11.** For the scenario: Use a fed-batch profile and a Michaelis\u2013Menten curve to infer where substrate dosing produces diminishing returns. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 12.** Evaluate the restriction: Enzyme denaturation, product inhibition and variable biomass cannot always be represented by constant Vmax. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 13.** For electrochemical and photocatalytic reactors, derive or interpret `n_product=I t FE/(zF)` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 14.** Design an experiment to distinguish current-based conversion, photon limits and performance accounting from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 15.** For the scenario: Vary current and Faradaic efficiency for a laboratory electrolyzer to calculate production rate and specific electrical energy. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 16.** Evaluate the restriction: Do not interpret a Faraday-law upper bound as a complete electrochemical design calculation. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n## Marking guide\n\nEach answer receives credit for (i) a stated physical model and boundary (25%), (ii) correct balance and dimensional consistency (30%), (iii) justified parameter handling and numerical or graphical evidence (25%), and (iv) limitations and safety reasoning (20%). Students should not earn full marks for a numerical answer with no assumptions or units."
