          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: RTD, Non-Ideal Mixing Models and Scale-Up",
          "content": "# Unit 8 Project \u2014 RTD, Nonideal Reactors and Scale-Up\n\n## Deliverable\n\nCreate an engineering notebook that compares at least two models for **diagnosing mixing and validating predictive reactor models**. Define a physical system, specify operating inputs and use either published synthetic benchmark data or explicitly labeled generated observations. Identify which data are used to fit the model and reserve a separate condition for validation. Report the parameter values, uncertainty assumptions, plots, conservation residuals and a reasoned design recommendation.\n\n## Minimum experimental plan\n\nSelect three substantially different input conditions. Make a conservation-balance prediction before executing the numerical model. For each condition, draw the expected qualitative trend and state a testable mechanism. Compute or measure a reference state, then vary an operating input \u00b115%. Do not modify more than one independent variable for the first sensitivity analysis. If the model fails an invariant check, document the failure before attempting to tune parameters.\n\n## Model comparison and report\n\nCompare the simplest physically defensible relationship against a more detailed alternative. Explain which added parameter is supported by evidence and whether that parameter is identifiable independently. Use the lab files as exploratory tools, but calculate at least one benchmark by hand or by independent Python calculation. Submit a one-page executive conclusion supported by labeled graphs, not a screenshot alone.\n\n## Interpretation and safety\n\nEvaluate the operating changes that improve conversion while worsening pressure drop, hot-spot temperature, residence-time variance or stability, as relevant to this unit. Document at least two failure modes of the proposed model. Conclude with one experiment that would most efficiently reduce uncertainty before anyone considered a physical scale-up. Classroom results must not be used to establish process-safety limits or authorize industrial operation."
