          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: RTD, Non-Ideal Mixing Models and Scale-Up",
          "content": "# Unit 8 Assessment \u2014 RTD, Nonideal Reactors and Scale-Up\n\n**16 topic-specific questions.** Provide equations, units, diagrams, uncertainty and interpretation rather than only one final number.\n\n**Question 1.** For residence-time distribution measurements, derive or interpret `integral_0^infinity E(t)dt=1` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 2.** Design an experiment to distinguish pulse tracer E(t), mean age and variance from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 3.** For the scenario: Compare normalized impulse distributions of one and four tanks in series and evaluate the fraction of material leaving before half the mean residence time. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 4.** Evaluate the restriction: A detector dead time or nonconservative tracer can distort normalization. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 5.** For tanks-in-series and axial dispersion, derive or interpret `sigma_theta^2=1/N` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 6.** Design an experiment to distinguish mixing spectrum, dimensionless variance and effective axial transport from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 7.** For the scenario: Fit a tanks-in-series value to a measured normalized variance and contrast predicted conversion with PFR and CSTR bounds for first-order kinetics. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 8.** Evaluate the restriction: A fitted RTD alone does not identify micromixing for nonlinear reactions. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 9.** For segregation and micromixing, derive or interpret `X_seg=integral[1-exp(-k t)] E(t)dt` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 10.** Design an experiment to distinguish mixing limits for nonlinear kinetics from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 11.** For the scenario: Use identical residence-time distributions but different kinetics orders to show why RTD-derived predictions need a micromixing assumption. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 12.** Evaluate the restriction: Do not apply linear first-order analytical results to high-order autocatalytic reactions without derivation. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n**Question 13.** For validation, scale-up and safety boundaries, derive or interpret `error=observation-model_prediction` and define the measurement basis and physical range of the inputs.\n\n**Question 14.** Design an experiment to distinguish identifiability, uncertainty and design verification from an alternate kinetic or transport explanation. Include the controlled quantity, instrument and expected trend.\n\n**Question 15.** For the scenario: Propose a holdout experiment varying space velocity and inlet temperature, and report predicted conversion with an interval reflecting parameter uncertainty. Set up the conservation balance, state approximations and explain what observation would contradict them.\n\n**Question 16.** Evaluate the restriction: Peer review, hazard analysis and plant-specific code compliance are separate from a successful classroom calculation. Identify a consequence of ignoring it and suggest an additional measurement.\n\n## Marking guide\n\nEach answer receives credit for (i) a stated physical model and boundary (25%), (ii) correct balance and dimensional consistency (30%), (iii) justified parameter handling and numerical or graphical evidence (25%), and (iv) limitations and safety reasoning (20%). Students should not earn full marks for a numerical answer with no assumptions or units."
